summary:
- topic: FY2027 Budget Crisis Overview
  text: The Administration, led by Dr. Johanna Prince, presented the district's major
    financial challenge for the FY2027 budget. A combination of declining student
    enrollment, rising staffing levels, increased costs, and a depleted fund balance
    has created a projected $7.2 million budget gap. Maintaining current services
    would require an 18.95% tax increase, but the board has set a target ceiling of
    6%, necessitating significant cuts.
- topic: Proposed 'Big Ideas' for Savings
  text: 'To begin addressing the shortfall, the administration introduced three preliminary,
    large-scale ideas for consideration: reconfiguring elementary schools into grade-banded
    centers (saving ~$2M), adjusting secondary school schedules and staffing (saving
    ~$1M), and finding operational efficiencies (saving ~$1M). It was emphasized that
    these are not formal proposals and that even if all were implemented, a gap of
    over $3 million would remain.'
- topic: Citizen and Board Q&A
  text: During the Q&A session, citizens and board members raised concerns about the
    potential impacts of the ideas. Key questions focused on how reconfiguration would
    affect busing costs, what the new average class sizes would be (projected to rise
    from ~16 to 18-23), the justification for a projected 12% health insurance increase,
    and the status of a new education foundation, which the administration clarified
    is not intended to solve the operating budget crisis.
- topic: Rationale for Strategic Choices
  text: The administration explained why certain options were not on the table for
    FY2027. Consolidating middle school start times was deemed logistically unfeasible
    for the upcoming school year due to permitting and traffic study timelines. Closing
    a school building was also ruled out, as the district anticipates needing the
    space to accommodate universal pre-K and new state mandates for special education
    services for children aged 3-5.
- topic: Superintendent Search Engagement
  text: A Board member made a direct appeal to the community for greater participation
    in the ongoing search for a new superintendent. She noted that recent virtual
    feedback sessions had extremely low attendance (only three people) and urged taxpayers
    beyond the immediate school community to provide input to ensure the hiring process
    reflects the entire city's priorities.
- topic: Next Steps and Public Feedback
  text: The administration outlined the upcoming budget timeline, including board
    workshops in March. A new public Q&A system using a Google Form was introduced
    to streamline questions and post answers publicly. Attendees were encouraged to
    provide anonymous feedback on the forum and the budget ideas via an exit survey.
